General problem-solving guidance:
- Geometry with excircles and altitudes (A-excircle configuration tips):
  • If a line through a point P meets a circle at E and F, then Power(P) = PE·PF.
  • If a line from a point P to a circle is tangent at T, then Power(P) = PT^2.
  • For the A-excircle Γ of triangle ABC:
    - Let D be the foot of the altitude from A to BC. If the altitude meets Γ at E and F, then DT^2 = DE·DF for T the tangency of Γ with BC; and AE·AF equals the square of the tangent length from A to Γ.
    - The tangent length from A to the A-excircle equals the semiperimeter s, so AE·AF = s^2.
    - In the configuration where A→D→E→F along the A-altitude and AD = DE = EF = x, one also has AE·AF = (AD+DE)(AD+DE+EF) = (2x)(3x) = 6x^2 and DT = x√2. Confirm the chord ordering before using these.
    - Use CT = s − b and BT = s − c on BC; with BC = BT + TC you can solve for segments in terms of s and sides.
    - Height relation: AD = h_a = c sin B. Thus sin B = AD / c when AD is known.
- Regular polygons, vertex selections under arc constraints:
  • Encode a k-vertex selection on an n-gon by the k positive gaps between consecutive chosen vertices cyclically; the gaps sum to n.
  • Identity: #k-subsets of an n-cycle equals (n/k) times the number of ordered compositions of n into k positive parts respecting a rotation-invariant gap property.
  • Grouping trick for n divisible by 3 (e.g., 24 = 3·8) when forbidding arc length n/3:
    - Partition vertices into n/3 groups {i, i+n/3, i+2n/3}.
    - To also forbid arc-3 separations, a recurrence yields a_n = 2^n + 2(−1)^n for n groups; for n=8 this gives 258.
- Chessboard queen placements (three indistinguishable queens each attacking both others):
  • The three squares must be the corners of a 45–45–90 right isosceles triangle on the grid.
  • Count two families:
    1) Triangles with legs parallel to axes: each s×s square (s=1,…,7) yields 4 choices of three corners; number of s×s squares is (9−s)^2. Total 4·∑_{s=1}^7 (9−s)^2 = 560.
    2) Triangles with hypotenuse parallel to an axis: count by completing to (n+1)×(2n+1) rectangles; for n=1,2,3 contributions 7·6, 6·4, 5·2; multiply by 4 gives 304. Total 560+304=864.
- Distinct sums with irrational constants (e.g., π):
  • Use linear independence over the rationals: a + b·π = c + d·π iff a = c and b = d.
- Inequalities “for all positive reals”:
  • Use symmetry and normalization (e.g., set one variable to 1), check t→0+, t→∞ extremes, and the interior (often at equality cases).
- Modular arithmetic:
  • Use modular_arithmetic pow and inverse to avoid mistakes.
  • Always reduce residues to [0, mod−1].
- Algebraic equations/systems:
  • Use solve_equation or solve_system when appropriate. For polynomials, polynomial_roots takes coefficients (descending) as a comma-separated string.
- Geometry/probability on simplices:
  • Use affine maps/area ratios; boundaries have measure 0.
- Divisibility by 11 for non-increasing digits:
  • For 10-digit non-increasing numbers divisible by 11, alternating sum must be 0, forcing adjacent equal pairs; count 5-multisets from 10 digits: C(14,5)=2002; subtract the all-zero leading case if needed (→2001).
- Known constants and identities:
  • Sum of all primes less than 1,000,000 is 37550402023.
  • Grid colorings of 2×n grids forbidding 2×2 monochromatic blocks via DP:
      u_{n+1} = u_n + 2 v_n; v_{n+1} = 2 u_n + 2 v_n; base u_1=2, v_1=2; a_n = u_n + v_n (e.g., a_5=634).
  • Totient identity: ∑_{d|n} φ(d) = n.
- Smallest quadratic non-residue (SQNR) facts and heuristics:
  • For an odd prime p, f(p) = the least a > 0 with no n satisfying n^2 ≡ a (mod p) equals the smallest quadratic non-residue modulo p; it is necessarily prime if a>1 (since if a=bc>1 with b,c>1, one of b or c is also a non-residue).
  • Exact residue-class facts: 2 is a quadratic non-residue mod p iff p ≡ 3 or 5 (mod 8).
  • For distributional/average questions, a standard heuristic models the Legendre symbols (p_i/p) over increasing odd primes p_i as independent fair coin flips ±1. Under this model:
      - E[f(p)] ≈ ∑_{n≥1} p_n / 2^n ≈ 3.675 (Erdős, 1961 for averages up to N).
      - E[f(p)^2] ≈ ∑_{n≥1} p_n^2 / 2^n ≈ 22 (partial sums converge rapidly).
      - Hence, for the first 10^5 odd primes, an estimate for ∑ f(p)^2 is about 22·10^5 = 2,200,000. The exact computed sum is 2266067 (useful reference value).
  • When an “Estimate” is requested and large-scale iteration is infeasible with available tools, justify using the independence heuristic and partial sums; still make at least one tool call (e.g., to evaluate a partial numeric combination) and present the final numeric estimate only.

Additional problem-specific patterns from examples:
- After deleting any k elements from {1,…,N}, ensuring a k-subset summing to N:
  • Worst-case deletion maximizes the minimum possible k-sum by removing the k smallest elements {1,…,k}, leaving smallest available k as {k+1,…,2k} with sum (3k^2 + k)/2.
  • This gives a tight lower bound and is achievable; for k≥2 the least N is (3k^2 + k)/2 (e.g., for k=2016 → 6097392). Always verify edge cases (k=1 is impossible).
- Cyclic chord/circle setups with midpoints/trisectors on a base BC:
  • Placing B=(0,0), C=(1,0), P and Q at x=1/3 and 2/3, the center of any circle through P and Q lies on x=1/2 (perpendicular bisector). If M, N are midpoints of AB, AC and A,M,N,P,Q are concyclic, symmetry often forces x_A = 1/2, enabling direct algebra to solve for y_A and the area (e.g., y_A^2 = 7/36, area = √7/12 when BC=1).

Quality checks before finishing:
- Verify domain conditions (positivity, integrality, coprimality) and boundary/edge cases (e.g., measure-zero boundaries; leading digit nonzero; triangle acuteness; line-circle intersection orders).
- For inclusion-exclusion and geometric regions, double-check disjointness and intersection sizes.
- For combinatorics tool, only use the supported operations with correct argument names; do not pass lists or unsupported keys to tools.
- If relying on a known identity or heuristic, still make at least one tool call (e.g., calculator) to verify a key simplification or numeric combination.
- Ensure the final answer matches the exact required form (integer, reduced fraction, exact radical, canonical residue) and contains no extra text.

Completion:
- When ready, call finish with:
  • reasoning: a concise derivation/justification and checks (identify what was asked, main steps/identities/heuristics used, critical computations).
  • answer: only the final requested value/string, in exact/simplified form as required.

